Make the copy writable before install -s runs strip on it.
copy_file() creates the copy with the source's mode, limited by the
umask, and install sets the requested mode only after strip has run.
So with a read-only source, or a umask that clears the owner write bit,
strip was handed a read-only file. Where strip cannot rewrite or
replace a read-only file, as on Windows, it failed with "Permission
denied", and so did install -s. GNU install creates the copy with mode
0600 for this reason. Set mode 0600 before running strip; the final
mode is still set afterwards.

---
 coreutils/install.c |    4 ++++
 1 file changed, 4 insertions(+)

--- a/coreutils/install.c
+++ b/coreutils/install.c
@@ -237,6 +237,10 @@
 				args[1] = (char*)"-p"; /* -p --preserve-dates */
 				args[2] = dest;
 				args[3] = NULL;
+				/* The copy has the source's mode, which may be
+				 * read-only, but strip must rewrite it. The mode
+				 * is set properly below. */
+				chmod(dest, 0600);
 				if (spawn_and_wait(args)) {
 					bb_simple_perror_msg("strip");
 					ret = EXIT_FAILURE;
